From d602c2f9e829c5a2d528061ed06480cf6c451711 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rolando Trevino <rolotrealanis@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 20:53:25 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 19/33] mfd: macsmc: give up on an atomic SMC write after
 SMC_TIMEOUT_MS

apple_smc_write_atomic() is what macsmc-reboot uses for power-off (and
restart where it is allowed): it takes the spinlock with interrupts off,
sends the write and polls RTKit until the reply clears atomic_pending.
There was no limit. If the SMC never answers (crashed, wedged, or a
firmware that ignores the key), the CPU spins with interrupts disabled
forever: the machine freezes with no oops and no watchdog message, which
is the failure shape we saw in round 3.

Stop after SMC_TIMEOUT_MS (5000 polls of 100 us), clear atomic_pending
and return -ETIMEDOUT, so the caller falls through to the next power-off
or restart handler (the watchdog for restart).

This is a safety bound, not a behaviour change when the SMC replies.

Signed-off-by: Rolando Trevino <rolotrealanis@gmail.com>

---
 drivers/mfd/macsmc.c | 11 ++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/mfd/macsmc.c b/drivers/mfd/macsmc.c
index 48f4020b2..1661fcf9b 100644
--- a/drivers/mfd/macsmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mfd/macsmc.c
@@ -283,7 +283,16 @@ int apple_smc_write_atomic(struct apple_smc *smc, smc_key key, const void *buf,
 		return ret;
 	}
 
-	while (smc->atomic_pending) {
+	/*
+	 * This runs with interrupts off (power-off, reboot), so a reply that
+	 * never comes must not spin forever: give up after SMC_TIMEOUT_MS.
+	 */
+	for (unsigned int polls = 0; smc->atomic_pending; polls++) {
+		if (polls >= SMC_TIMEOUT_MS * 10) {
+			smc->atomic_pending = false;
+			dev_err(smc->dev, "Atomic command timed out (%llx)\n", msg);
+			return -ETIMEDOUT;
+		}
 		ret = apple_rtkit_poll(smc->rtk);
 		if (ret < 0) {
 			dev_err(smc->dev, "RTKit poll failed (%llx)", msg);
-- 
